-------------------------------------------------------------------
Fri Nov  6 09:48:28 UTC 2026 - Johannes Kastl <opensuse_buildservice@ojkastl.de>

- update to 11.28.11:
  pnpm 11.28.11 fixes frozen installs with
  excludeLinksFromLockfile, pnpm link in workspaces, pnpm deploy
  with dangling symlinks, and verifyDepsBeforeRun starting needless
  installs.
  * Patch Changes
    - Installing packages
      - pnpm deploy now keeps a symlink in the project whose target
        is missing from the source, such as a link into a
        node_modules directory that was removed after the build.
        Deploy used to fail with ENOENT on such a link #17571.
      - A package with bundledDependencies and a build script now
        keeps its bundled dependencies when it is restored from the
        side-effects cache #17523.
      - pnpm install now links the runtime that an earlier pnpm
        install --no-runtime skipped #17542.
      - Commands of a package whose bin paths contain excess parent
        directory segments, such as ../dist/cli.js, are no longer
        missing. Such paths now resolve inside the package #17586.
      - Generated POSIX shell shims now quote their paths, so shell
        special characters in a path stay literal.
      - A repeat pnpm install now rewrites the bin shims after
        extendNodePath changes. Before, it reported "Already up to
        date" and the shims kept the NODE_PATH of the previous
        setting.
    - Lockfile and resolution
      - pnpm install --frozen-lockfile now accepts the lockfile it
        wrote with excludeLinksFromLockfile and
        linkWorkspacePackages on, when a project depends on a
        workspace package through a plain version range or a
        catalog: entry. It used to fail with
        ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE.
      - pnpm install --frozen-lockfile, pnpm approve-builds, and
        pnpm rebuild now accept a lockfile that resolves a *
        dependency to a prerelease latest version. pnpm wrote such
        a lockfile and then reported it as outdated #17594.
      - Packages from the default registry keep plain lockfile
        keys, even when a prefix such as npmjs points at the
        registry set as registry. A dependency written with such a
        prefix shares the plain entry of the same package. The next
        install rewrites keys that earlier versions qualified this
        way #17575.
      - Relative local paths in root overrides now resolve from the
        workspace root when each project has its own lockfile
        #17691.
      - Installing a local directory dependency no longer fails
        when another project's install is writing a lockfile in
        that directory.
      - Peer deduplication no longer gives a project different
        copies of a dependency across direct and transitive paths
        when an optional peer has no provider #17666.
      - A Node.js version range such as >=26.13, for example in
        devEngines.runtime, now resolves to the newest stable
        release that satisfies it. pnpm picks a prerelease only
        when no stable release matches #17516.
    - verifyDepsBeforeRun
      - With verifyDepsBeforeRun, pnpm run and pnpm exec now report
        dependencies as out of date after a dependency is added to
        the package.json of a local directory dependency, such as
        "lib": "file:../lib". Before, the command ran without the
        new dependency installed.
      - verifyDepsBeforeRun no longer reports dependencies as out
        of sync when only the version field of a single-project
        repository changed #17560. The same holds for a workspace
        project that no dependency can resolve to. A script run
        after such a change no longer starts an install first.
    - pnpm link
      - In a workspace, pnpm link <dir> now adds the link:
        dependency to the package.json of the project where the
        command runs, not to the workspace root's package.json. The
        link is created in that project's node_modules, as
        documented. The override is still written to the root
        pnpm-workspace.yaml.
      - pnpm link with several paths now records every linked
        package in the overrides of pnpm-workspace.yaml. Some of
        them could be missing before.
      - pnpm link now fails without changing package.json or
        pnpm-workspace.yaml when the linked package's name is not a
        valid npm package name. It used to write that name to both
        files, which made every later install fail #17475.
        pnpm  -  update
      - pnpm update with only negated selectors, such as pnpm
        update '!foo', now leaves the dependencies listed in
        update.ignoreDeps unchanged pnpm/tasks#243.
      - pnpm update --depth 0 without selectors now does nothing
        when every direct dependency is listed in
        update.ignoreDeps. Before, it failed with
        ERR_PNPM_NO_PACKAGE_IN_DEPENDENCIES pnpm/tasks#251.
    - Store and self-update
      - pnpm store prune now keeps packages linked from a
        configured modules directory pnpm/tasks#230.
      - pnpm store status no longer reports untouched packages as
        modified when the global virtual store is enabled. If the
        installed global-store layout cannot be verified, the
        command reports ERR_PNPM_STORE_STATUS_UNVERIFIABLE and asks
        for a reinstall #17631.
      - pnpm store add now fails right away with
        ERR_PNPM_FROZEN_STORE_WRITE when frozenStore is enabled. It
        used to retry each download for minutes before failing, or
        fail with a SQLite error on an empty store.
      - pnpm self-update now reinstalls a cached engine if its
        executable cannot run. This repairs shared store entries
        damaged by an earlier install #17582.
- update to 11.28.10:
  pnpm 11.28.10 stops pnpm store prune from deleting other stores
  when cacheDir points to their parent directory. It also fixes
  globally installed commands that load hoisted dependencies, and
  repeat installs that missed changes to local dependencies.
  * Patch Changes
    - Installing packages
      - pnpm store prune preserves other stores and global package
        data when cacheDir points to their parent directory.
      - Commands installed with pnpm add --global now find the
        dependencies that pnpm hoists for their package. A package
        that loads a dependency it does not declare, such as a
        native addon's platform-specific package, failed with
        "Cannot find module". Reinstall such a package with pnpm
        add --global to update its existing command #17503.
      - pnpm prune --prod no longer fails with ERR_PNPM_EXDEV when
        node_modules was copied from an earlier Docker build stage
        #17424.
      - pnpm install now retries a Node.js runtime zip download
        when the connection drops partway through. A download that
        still fails names its URL. Before, a single dropped
        connection ended the install with terminated #17411.
      - With the global virtual store, a change to a dependency of
        one package in a dependency cycle now gives every package
        in that cycle a new directory. Before, the other packages
        kept their directories and still linked the old copy of the
        changed package, so the project could load two copies of it
        #17466.
        Packages in a dependency cycle, and packages that depend on
        one, move to new global virtual store directories once
        after this update. Those that run a build miss the
        side-effects cache once.
      - pnpm install and pnpm rebuild now refresh privately hoisted
        .bin shims in node_modules/.pnpm/node_modules/.bin after a
        dependency build replaces a script target with a native
        executable #17402.
      - The Node.js latest selector now chooses the newest stable
        release #17490.
      - The standalone @pnpm/exe builds now embed Node.js 26.13.0.
    - Repeat installs and lockfiles
      - pnpm install now resolves a local directory again after its
        package.json changes when an override or a package
        extension adds it to the dependencies of a registry
        package. pnpm install --frozen-lockfile fails on a lockfile
        that still records the old dependencies of such a directory
        #17486.
      - With injectWorkspacePackages and per-project lockfiles,
        pnpm install now updates a project's lockfile when a local
        tarball that an injected workspace project depends on is
        replaced. The project's lockfile kept the old version and
        integrity before.
      - pnpm install now detects a change to a local tarball or
        directory that only a pnpmfile readPackage hook depends on.
        A repeat install reported "Already up to date" and kept the
        old contents before pnpm/tasks#227.
      - pnpm install now relinks a workspace: or link: dependency
        whose link was deleted from node_modules. It used to report
        "Already up to date" and leave the link missing #17485.
      - Merging git branch lockfiles now keeps a workspace project
        whose directory is named __proto__. It was dropped from the
        merged lockfile before pnpm/tasks#59.
    - Adding and publishing packages
      - pnpm add no longer rewrites a catalog entry set to the
        dist-tag that the dependency resolves through. For example,
        an entry beta stays beta after pnpm add foo@beta or pnpm
        add foo. Before, the entry was replaced with the version
        the tag pointed to pnpm/tasks#225.
      - pnpm pack and pnpm publish now always include the binary
        file declared by a string bin field in package.json, even
        when files or .npmignore would otherwise exclude it.
      - When the version or range given to pnpm unpublish matches
        every published version, the error now says so and shows
        the same command with --force. It suggested running pnpm
        unpublish <pkg>@<version>, the command that had just
        failed.
    - Running commands
      - Every command, including pnpm --version, pnpm self-update,
        and invocations with unknown flags, now applies the same
        packageManager and devEngines.packageManager settings when
        switching to the pinned pnpm version or recording it in
        pnpm-lock.yaml. --config.<key> flags and lockfileDir now
        reach the version switch and lockfile sync on every entry
        point. Unreadable settings in pnpm-workspace.yaml or the
        global config.yaml that do not affect the pin decision no
        longer block the version switch pnpm/tasks#222.
      - pnx and pnpm create now run the command in the directory
        selected by --dir or --workspace-root. Without either flag,
        the command still runs in the directory pnpm was started
        in.
      - The error for a dependency with an invalid name in a
        project's manifest now names the project's directory. It
        used to say only "The current package", which did not tell
        which workspace project to fix #17475.
- update to 11.28.9:
  This release fixes a crash on registry responses with too many
  content encodings (GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c) and sizes the install
  worker pool by the container's CPU quota. A repeat pnpm install
  now acts on more setting changes.
  * Patch Changes
    - Crashes and security
      - A registry response with too many content encodings can no
        longer crash pnpm. pnpm now uses undici 7.30.1, which fixes
        GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c.
      - pnpm install now sizes its worker pool by the CPU quota of
        the container it runs in, such as a Kubernetes CPU limit,
        when that quota is smaller than the number of CPUs the
        container may run on. The default workspaceConcurrency
        respects the quota the same way. On a CI runner with a
        4-CPU quota and hundreds of host CPUs, pnpm started
        hundreds of workers and ran out of memory #17382.
    - Repeat installs and changed settings
      - A repeat pnpm install now acts on a change to these
        settings. Before, it reported "Already up to date".
        - storeDir or virtualStoreDir: it recreates node_modules.
        - virtualStoreDirMaxLength: it renames the virtual store directories.
        - dangerouslyAllowAllBuilds turned on: it runs the builds it skipped.
        - trustLockfile turned off: it verifies the lockfile.
      - pnpm add, pnpm remove, and pnpm update now fail when the
        node-linker setting differs from the one recorded in
        node_modules. A pnpm install of the whole workspace
        recreates node_modules with the new node-linker, and an
        install filtered to some projects fails pnpm/tasks#203.
      - An install filtered to some workspace projects now fails if
        node_modules was installed with a different nodeLinker,
        hoistPattern, publicHoistPattern, store, or virtual store.
        Before, it recreated node_modules and left the projects it
        skipped without their dependencies. A pnpm install of the
        whole workspace still recreates node_modules
        pnpm/tasks#217.
      - pnpm install now takes its fast path in a workspace with
        patchedDependencies when the previous install ran with
        another major version of pnpm.
      - pnpm install now relinks the bins of a workspace package
        when its bin field changes, including with
        --frozen-lockfile and an unchanged lockfile. A filtered
        install relinks them in the selected projects, and the next
        install that covers the remaining projects relinks them
        there #17324.
      - pnpm install now removes command shims from
        node_modules/.bin when a workspace dependency renames or
        removes an entry from its bin field pnpm/tasks#211.
      - With nodeLinker: hoisted, an install that stops partway no
        longer leaves the next install trusting the packages it was
        replacing. Before, reverting the change that started the
        stopped install, such as a patch edit, kept the
        half-replaced package in node_modules.
      - pnpm run and pnpm exec no longer report the dependencies as
        out of date after a lockfile change when dedupePeers is
        true #17322.
    - Resolving dependencies and the lockfile
      - A package that a plain range and a <prefix>: specifier both
        resolve from one registry is now recorded once in
        pnpm-lock.yaml and installed once. The lockfile key names
        the registry a package resolved from. When that registry
        declares a prefix, through prefix in registries or through
        namedRegistries, the package is keyed
        <name>@<prefix>:<version> however its dependency was
        written: with the prefix, as a plain range its scope routes
        to that registry, or through the default registry. Before,
        the plain range was keyed <name>@<version>, so one tarball
        was installed twice and instanceof checks and module
        singletons broke between the two copies. The same name and
        version served by two registries still get separate
        entries. When several prefixes declare one registry, its
        packages are keyed by the lexicographically smallest
        prefix.
        If a scope you route reaches a registry that also declares
        a prefix, your next pnpm install re-keys those entries,
        which shows up as a lockfile diff. Commit it. pnpm install
        --frozen-lockfile installs the lockfile as recorded.
      - pnpm install now resolves a scope's packages again when the
        scope is routed to another registry in registries. Before,
        the lockfile kept the versions and integrity resolved from
        the old registry, so the install failed when the new
        registry did not serve a locked version or served different
        content. pnpm records in the workspace state which registry
        each scope routed to, so a fresh clone without that record
        installs the lockfile as recorded. Changing the default
        registry resolves nothing again, and pnpm install
        --frozen-lockfile is unchanged.
      - An optional dependency written with a named-registry
        prefix, such as work:^1.0.0, now fails the install when it
        cannot be resolved but the lockfile already has a matching
        entry from that registry. Such a dependency was skipped,
        which removed its entries from the lockfile.
      - With injectWorkspacePackages: true, a workspace: dependency
        written as a relative path, such as workspace:../lib-a, in
        a workspace package now resolves from that package's
        directory #17284.
      - Fixed pnpm install failing with
        ERR_PNPM_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL when a workspace package
        injected through a relative path, such as workspace:../lib,
        has catalog: dependencies. Their catalog entries now
        resolve the way they do for a package injected by name
        #17280.
      - pnpm install now applies the serverType registry setting
        when it updates the lockfile for an override or catalog
        change without a full resolution. pnpm wrote the tarball
        URLs of a declared registry into the lockfile in that case.
      - Added the pnpr value to the serverType registry setting.
        pnpm leaves the tarball URLs of a pnpr registry out of
        pnpm-lock.yaml.
      - A registries entry whose prefix is __proto__ is no longer
        ignored.
      - Installs delegated to a pnpr server now resolve every
        prefix declared for a registry URL. A dependency or
        lockfile entry that used the second of two prefixes for the
        same registry, such as zeta: next to work: in
        namedRegistries, failed on the server pnpm/tasks#204.
    - Registries, proxies, and downloading pnpm
      - The proxy settings httpProxy, httpsProxy, noProxy, proxy,
        and noproxy set in the global config.yaml, through
        PNPM_CONFIG_HTTPS_PROXY and the matching variables, or as
        --config.httpsProxy on the command line now apply to pnpm
        self-update and to the download of the pnpm version pinned
        in packageManager. Previously, these requests read the
        proxy only from .npmrc and the HTTPS_PROXY family of
        variables pnpm/tasks#215.
      - When https-proxy is false, pnpm now downloads the pnpm
        version pinned by packageManager through the legacy proxy
        setting, as installs already do. Setting proxy to false now
        also stops that download from using the HTTP_PROXY
        environment variable. The signature check of the pinned
        pnpm now uses the same proxy and TLS settings as its
        download pnpm/tasks#193.
      - strictSsl and localAddress set in the global config.yaml,
        through PNPM_CONFIG_STRICT_SSL and
        PNPM_CONFIG_LOCAL_ADDRESS, or on the command line now apply
        to pnpm self-update and to the download of the pnpm version
        pinned in packageManager. These requests read them from
        .npmrc only before. --config.localAddress now reaches them
        in the camelCase spelling too pnpm/tasks#201.
    - Commands
      - pnpm audit --ignore and pnpm audit --ignore-unfixable now
        exit with a non-zero code when vulnerabilities at or above
        the audit level remain after ignoring. Before, these
        commands always exited with code 0.
      - pnpm audit --fix and pnpm audit --interactive now fail with
        ERR_PNPM_AUDIT_FIX_WITH_IGNORE when combined with --ignore
        or --ignore-unfixable. Before, the fix ran and the ignore
        flags had no effect.
      - pnpm ci --lockfile and pnpm ci --config.lockfile=true no
        longer delete pnpm-lock.yaml. pnpm ci now always keeps the
        lockfile and runs a frozen install from it.
      - pnpm deploy now keeps a deployed symlink to a directory
        inside the project even if the directory is not among the
        deployed files, such as a link into node_modules #17288.
      - pnpm pack and pnpm publish again include the files of a
        publishConfig.directory that is reached through a symlink,
        together with its bundled dependencies #17390.
      - pnpm -r publish --batch now sends PUT /-/pnpr/v0/publish,
        the batch publish endpoint that pnpr serves.
      - pnpm store prune now keeps the packages that a project
        still uses when pnpm copied or cloned them into
        node_modules. These include patched packages, built
        packages, and every package installed with
        packageImportMethod: copy. Before, the prune removed them,
        and the next install downloaded them again #7192.
      - pnpm store prune now keeps the packages of a project that
        was installed with a different symlinked spelling of the
        store path. Previously, prune dropped such a project from
        its list and removed the packages only that project used
        from the global virtual store.
    - Speed and output
      - Sped up installs of projects whose local directory
        dependencies share transitive local directory dependencies.
        pnpm no longer checks a shared directory again for each
        direct dependency that reaches it #17374.
      - Output that fills the terminal, such as pnpm run --stream
        script output, is now written once. Before, every new line
        also rewrote all the lines already on screen #17387.
      - The warning about unrecognized settings now marks
        symlinkedLockfileTargetDirs as a setting of a newer pnpm
        version.
- update to 11.28.8:
  * Patch Changes
    - The standalone @pnpm/exe builds now embed Node.js 26.12.1.
      The previously embedded Node.js 26.0.0 still trusted the
      certificate authority intermediates that Node.js distrusted
      in October 2026, so a standalone pnpm accepted TLS
      certificates issued under them.
- update to 11.28.7:
  This release rejects registry metadata that names a different
  package than the one requested, applies trustPolicy to the pnpm
  version a project pins, and caps unpacked package size with
  maxTarballUnpackedSize. It also removes support for registry
  revisions, fixes verifyDepsBeforeRun after filtered installs, and
  finishes commands with their own exit code when a pipe reader
  exits early.
  * Patch Changes
    - Security
      - pnpm now fails with ERR_PNPM_REGISTRY_NAME_MISMATCH if a
        registry returns metadata for a package other than the one
        requested. Before, pnpm installed the package under the
        name in the metadata, so allowBuilds rules for the
        requested package did not apply to it.
      - pnpm now applies trustPolicy to the pnpm version a project
        pins in packageManager or devEngines.packageManager. A
        pinned version whose trust evidence was downgraded fails
        the command before pnpm switches to it or records it in the
        lockfile #16319.
      - minimumReleaseAgeExcludePrune and trustPolicyExcludePrune
        now keep exclusions for resolved configuration dependencies
        and their transitive dependencies #17142.
      - Added the maxTarballUnpackedSize setting. pnpm install
        fails with ERR_PNPM_TARBALL_TOO_LARGE when the files of one
        package unpack to more than this many bytes, 4 GiB by
        default pnpm/tasks#81.
    - Resolving dependencies
      - Registry revisions are no longer supported. pnpm now fails
        with ERR_PNPM_REGISTRY_REVISIONS_UNSUPPORTED on registry
        metadata that selects a revision, on a +rN version
        specifier, and on a lockfile entry with a revision field.
        pnpm update --patches is removed.
      - When pnpm install resolves a dependency again, it now
        reuses a version that the install already resolved for the
        same package, as a fresh install does. Before, it kept the
        version the lockfile pinned and added a second copy of the
        package. A locked version still stays when nothing else in
        the install prefers another version. To pin a transitive
        dependency on purpose, use overrides #17084.
      - When another workspace project adds a different version of
        a package, a project's auto-installed peer on that package
        now moves to the version a fresh install picks. It used to
        stay on its locked version while any other project still
        accepted that version #17058.
      - An optional peer that pnpm adds to a project now keeps the
        version the lockfile recorded for that project only while
        the dependency graph still holds it. Otherwise it follows
        its provider to a version the graph holds. Before, pnpm
        kept a higher version that only the lockfile held, which
        left two copies of the peer #16443.
      - A package that lists the same dependency in dependencies
        and as an optional peer now gets the peer bound when a
        project adds a provider for it later. The lockfile now
        records every peer a package declares, including one its
        own dependency supplies #17056.
      - pnpm install --prod no longer drops a dependency that a
        package declares both as an optional peer and as its own
        dependency, when the package resolved it as its own
        dependency and a dev dependency provides the same package
        under another alias.
      - pnpm now measures a pnpm.overrides entry written as a
        workspace: path, such as workspace:./packages/foo, from the
        directory holding pnpm-workspace.yaml. It used to be
        measured from each package the override rewrote. The
        install then linked a directory that does not exist, or
        failed if workspace packages were injected #17010.
      - pnpm install now removes config dependencies that are no
        longer declared, including when the entire
        configDependencies setting is removed. Frozen installs
        reject a lockfile that still records removed config
        dependencies.
      - pnpm add now reads the package name correctly from a
        tarball URL under a registry URL that has a path, such as
        https://example.com/npm/. Before, the registry path became
        part of the name, so a scoped package failed with
        ERR_PNPM_INVALID_PACKAGE_NAME.
      - pnpm add no longer warns that a gist dependency was
        replaced from a different source when the new specifier
        names the same gist in another form, such as gist:<id> and
        https://gist.github.com/<user>/<id>.
      - With minimumReleaseAge set, pnpm now revalidates a
        package's cached full metadata with a single ETag request
        so an unchanged registry answers with 304, and keeps using
        the cached copy once the registry confirms it is current,
        even when the metadata does not list a publish time for
        every version. Previously, pnpm downloaded that metadata
        again whenever the cached copy was older than
        minimumReleaseAge. Packages listed in
        minimumReleaseAgeExclude also resolve from cached metadata
        without a registry request when the lockfile already has
        the version their range picks.
    - Linking node_modules and workspaces
      - Concurrent installs that import the same package into one
        project now all complete. One of them used to fail with
        ENOTEMPTY when its copy of the package collided with the
        copy another install had already placed pnpm/tasks#48.
      - pnpm install no longer fails on macOS with EISDIR when it
        creates store directories in a group-writable store under a
        umask that removes owner read, such as 0o477.
      - pnpm install now succeeds when node_modules is a symlink to
        an existing directory. Dependency hoisting previously
        rejected a symlinked node_modules as an invalid destination
        #17157.
      - A filtered pnpm install --no-lockfile with
        hoistWorkspacePackages now hoists every workspace project
        into node_modules/.pnpm/node_modules. It used to hoist only
        the selected projects pnpm/tasks#168.
      - Filtered installs remove obsolete dependencies from the
        installed lockfile while preserving dependencies still
        needed by unselected projects.
      - With sharedWorkspaceLockfile: false, pnpm install now runs
        the preResolution hooks and custom resolvers and fetchers
        of the workspace root's pnpmfile for every project, next to
        the project's own. A global pnpmfile now runs once per
        project, and each project's lockfile records the checksum
        of the root pnpmfile when the project has none of its own.
        Before, a project's own pnpmfile replaced these root hooks,
        the global hooks ran twice, and edits to the root pnpmfile
        could go unnoticed.
      - pnpm prune now removes extraneous direct packages from
        node_modules when package.json and pnpm-lock.yaml are
        already in sync. pnpm prune also accepts --filter and
        --recursive to prune selected workspace projects without
        removing dependencies of unselected projects.
    - Injected workspace packages
      - pnpm install --frozen-lockfile now fails with
        ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE when the dependencies of an
        injected workspace package or local directory dependency no
        longer match the lockfile. This covers
        sharedWorkspaceLockfile: false #17008, a dependency changed
        to a different tarball URL or git commit #17129, and a
        change in a transitive injected or local directory
        dependency #17128.
      - pnpm install --frozen-lockfile no longer fails with
        ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE when an injected workspace
        package depends on a local directory under another name,
        such as "renamed": "file:../b" #17130.
      - An injected workspace dependency now becomes a link when
        its dependencies match the workspace package's own, also on
        an install that leaves the package's installed dependencies
        as they are. Before, such an install kept it as a copy
        #17094.
      - An injected workspace package that was linked now becomes a
        copy when the project that depends on it starts providing
        one of its peers at a version the package's own workspace
        context lacks. The link used to stay until that dependency
        was updated, so the package could not find the peer the
        project provides #17057.
      - An injected workspace package now keeps only the files its
        manifest lists after a lifecycle script or a script named
        in syncInjectedDepsAfterScripts rebuilds it. Before, these
        refreshes copied every file of the package directory into
        the injected copy, including files that files or .npmignore
        leave out. With deployAllFiles: true the copy still gets
        every file.
      - A script listed in syncInjectedDepsAfterScripts now keeps a
        package's links to its own files as links in the injected
        copies, the way pnpm install writes them. It no longer
        fails when the package links to one directory twice or
        links back to a directory above the link. pnpm install with
        deployAllFiles no longer fails on such a link back either.
      - A script listed in syncInjectedDepsAfterScripts now updates
        the injected copies of the package it ran in when
        sharedWorkspaceLockfile is false or the package publishes
        from publishConfig.directory. Before, those copies kept the
        files from the last install.
    - Running scripts and checking dependencies
      - When the reader of pnpm's output exits early, as in pnpm
        outdated | head, pnpm now finishes the command and exits
        with the command's own exit code pnpm/tasks#133. It used to
        exit with code 0 at the first write to the closed pipe.
        That hid the result of commands such as pnpm outdated and
        stopped pnpm clean before it removed anything.
      - With enableGlobalVirtualStore, a Node.js resolve hook that
        a script registers now sees the importer as
        context.parentURL after pnpm's NODE_PATH fallback fails to
        find a bare specifier. Hooks that resolve tsconfig paths
        aliases from the importer failed with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND
        #16979.
      - pnpm run, pnpm exec, and pnpm restart now pass the install
        flags they were given, such as --lockfile-dir,
        --modules-dir, --store-dir, --offline, and
        --ignore-scripts, to the pnpm install that
        verifyDepsBeforeRun: install starts. When that install
        leaves node_modules out of date, pnpm now prints a warning.
      - After pnpm install --filter, pnpm run and pnpm exec now
        notice a changed manifest or lockfile in a project that the
        filtered install left out, including the workspace root
        after a filtered install with pruneDirectDependencies: true
        pnpm/tasks#181. A filtered install also preserves the
        recorded ignored build scripts of unselected projects, and
        pnpm dedupe --check and pnpm install --dry-run no longer
        record outdated dependencies as installed.
      - The verifyDepsBeforeRun check now holds the workspace
        projects that the selected project depends on, including
        link: workspace dependencies. --filter=<project>... now
        selects link: dependencies as well. With
        sharedWorkspaceLockfile: false, verifyDepsBeforeRun also
        reports dependencies as outdated when a selected project's
        pnpm-lock.yaml is missing pnpm/tasks#138.
      - verifyDepsBeforeRun and repeat pnpm install runs now apply
        overrides before comparing a modified package.json with
        pnpm-lock.yaml #17127. verifyDepsBeforeRun also checks
        whether each importer's resolved catalog versions match the
        catalog snapshot in pnpm-lock.yaml, and respects
        lockfileDir in single-project repositories.
      - verifyDepsBeforeRun now compares manifests, lockfiles, and
        patch files by content hash. It notices an edit that kept
        the file's modification time, as cp -p, rsync -a, or an
        archive extraction leave it, and a patch file that was
        copied or touched without changing its content no longer
        marks dependencies as outdated #16988.
    - Deploying and other commands
      - pnpm deploy now records the peer dependencies of the
        workspace packages it copies into the deployed lockfile.
        Before, pnpm rejected that lockfile with --frozen-lockfile
        when a deployed workspace package declared a peer and
        injectWorkspacePackages was off #17093.
      - pnpm install --frozen-lockfile and verifyDepsBeforeRun now
        succeed inside a directory created by pnpm deploy. pnpm
        deploy writes self-contained workspace and lockfile
        settings, prunes unused patchedDependencies, and preserves
        workspace-level dependency resolutions on deployed
        directory dependencies.
      - In workspaces with sharedWorkspaceLockfile: false, pnpm
        patch, pnpm patch-commit, and pnpm patch-remove now resolve
        patch files and patchedDependencies entries at the
        workspace root. pnpm install checks configured patches
        across all workspace projects before reporting unused
        patches.
      - Trusted publishing with OIDC now works with registries
        whose URL has a path, such as
        https://registry.example/npm/private/. pnpm previously sent
        the token exchange request to the root of the registry
        host.
      - pnpm owner ls now lists the owners of a package on the npm
        registry. It reported every package as not found.
      - pnpm sbom now reads project lockfiles in workspaces with
        sharedWorkspaceLockfile: false, including filtered and
        split output, and reports the repository of a package whose
        repository field is a gist: shorthand.
      - pnpm licenses list reports no licenses for workspace
        projects not recorded in the lockfile. It errors when
        pnpm-lock.yaml is missing. Empty results now use the
        requested text or JSON format pnpm/tasks#174.
      - pnpm why now reads each selected workspace project's
        lockfile and modules directory when sharedWorkspaceLockfile
        is false pnpm/tasks#169.
      - pnpm pkg fix now converts a repository string such as
        github:pnpm/example to the object form, as npm pkg fix
        does. It also normalizes a GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket
        repository URL the way npm pkg fix does #16980.
    - Configuration and setting up pnpm
      - pnpm now recognizes externalDependencies and
        autoInstallPeersFromHighestMatch in pnpm-workspace.yaml
        without warning and forwards both settings to pnpm install.
        Previously, setting externalDependencies in
        pnpm-workspace.yaml with nodeLinker: hoisted crashed pnpm
        install with externalDependencies.has is not a function
        pnpm/tasks#166.
      - pnpm now ignores a PNPM_CONFIG_* environment variable for a
        URL setting, such as PNPM_CONFIG_REGISTRY, when its value
        is not a URL. Such a value used to make every command fail
        with Invalid URL.
      - The warning about unrecognized settings in
        pnpm-workspace.yaml and the global config.yaml now names
        addMissingPeerTypes, autoDedupe, bootConfig, macosBackup,
        nodePackageMap, permissions, pipelineBase,
        publishWaitTimeout, python, remoteCache, saveTypes, skills,
        storeIndexFormat, and tools as pnpm v12 settings
        pnpm/tasks#167.
      - When a config file has a setting pnpm cannot read, pnpm
        --version now prints the version, and pmOnFail and
        lockfileDir in pnpm-workspace.yaml stay in force.
        Previously, pnpm switched to the pinned version despite
        pmOnFail: ignore. pnpm also no longer writes the pinned
        version to pnpm-lock.yaml when it cannot read every setting
        and the running pnpm already matches the pin.
      - pnpm now records the pinned pnpm version in the
        pnpm-lock.yaml under the lockfileDir set in
        pnpm-workspace.yaml, and resolves a relative lockfileDir
        from the workspace root. pnpm used to write a second
        pnpm-lock.yaml at the workspace root.
      - pnpm self-update now updates the project's pnpm pin when
        Homebrew installed pnpm or Corepack runs it. The global
        pnpm is left alone #17001.
      - pnpm setup now completes when Windows registry environment
        values cannot be read or written. It prints PowerShell
        commands to configure PNPM_HOME and PATH without registry
        access #17167.
      - pnpm setup now succeeds when it cannot tell which shell
        configuration file to update, for example in a container
        where SHELL is not set. It installs pnpm and prints the
        PNPM_HOME and PATH lines to add yourself. Previously it
        failed with ERR_PNPM_UNKNOWN_SHELL, and so did the
        installer script at get.pnpm.io #16973.

